{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And a deep sleep fell upon Abram.</b> It fell, and during that sleep the Holy One, blessed be He, showed him the subjugation of the kingdoms and their rule and their destruction, as it is said: ״Take for me a three-year-old heifer״ — this is the fourth kingdom, which is harsh, as it is said: ״דא אילא ואימתא״ (this was a bear). ״עז״ — this is the kingdom of Greece, as it is said: ״והצעיר [וצפיר] הגדול מאד״. ״ואיל משולש״ — this is the kingdom of Media and Persia, as it is said: ״האיל אשר ראית בעל הקרנים מלכי מדי ופרס״. Our Rabbis taught: Rabbi Yishmael said: this 'tor' is nothing but in Aramaic — 'tor' is the ox. When the ox, male and female, pair up, they plow and lay waste all the valleys, as it is said: ״ארום חיויא רביעאה״ etc. ״And the bird״ — these are Israel, as it is said: ״יונתי בחגוי הסלע״, and it is written: ״אחת היא יונתי תמתי״. Rabbi Aḥa says: 'meshulash' is not said except in the sense of mighty ones, as: ״והחוט המשולש לא במהרה ינתק״. Rabbi Meshereshia says: they were tripled — three times to rule over the earth: first time each one by itself; second time two by two; third time all of them together warring against Mashiach ben David, as it is said: ״יתיצבו מלכי ארץ״. Rabbi Yehoshua said: He took each one's sword and split each one into two, as it is said: ״ויבתר אותם בתוך״, and immediately their strength failed — and had He not split them, the world could not have stood before them. ״ואת הצפור לא בתר״ — and it is a young dove, for there is no 'bird' written in the Torah except a young dove. ״וירד העיט על הפגרים״ — to feed them and destroy them. And 'eit' is nothing but the son of David, who is compared to a painted eagle, as it is said: ״העיט צבוע נחלתי לי״ — he appears calling from the east like an eagle at the sun's rising from the east. ״וישב אותם אברם״ — Abram was waving them with his garment, so that the eagle would not rule over them until evening. Rabbi Elazar said: from here you learn that the dominion of these four nations is nothing but one day of the Holy One, blessed be He. They said to Rabbi Elazar: indeed, according to your words — ״נתנני שוממה כל היום דוה״ — except for two watches of an hour. And know that this is so, for when the sun inclines to set in the west, its strength and its light fail until evening comes, when the light of Israel will spring up, as it is said: ״לעת ערב יהיה אור״. And there is further proof from here that the subjugation of the four kingdoms — this is the kingdom of Greece, which darkened the eyes of Israel from all the commandments in the Torah. ״גדולה זו מלכות מדי״ — this is the kingdom of Media, which grew exceedingly to sell Israel for nothing. ״נופלת עליו״ — this is the kingdom of Babylon, which caused the crown of Israel to fall into its hand. ״עליו״ — these are the children of Ishmael, upon whom the son of David will sprout, as it is said: ״אויביו אלביש בושת ועליו יציץ נזרו״. Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah says: these kingdoms were created only as firewood for Gehinnom, as it is said: ״והנה תנור עשן ולפיד אש״, and 'oven' is nothing but Gehinnom, as it is said: ״נאם ה' אשר אור לו בציון ותנור לו בירושלים״. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Abraham the subjugation of the kingdoms and Gehinnom, and showed him the Torah and the sacrifices. He said to him: as long as Israel engage in Torah and sacrifices, they will be saved from these two and will prosper. But the Temple is destined to be destroyed and the sacrifices to be abolished — what do you prefer, that your children be subjugated in the subjugation of the kingdoms or in the judgment of Gehinnom? And Abraham was astounded and did not know what to answer and choose. He said to him, as it were: why are you astounded? Choose for yourself the subjugation of the kingdoms, and let them not be judged in Gehinnom. Thus it is written: ״אם לא כי צורם מכרם וה' הסגירם״ — 'their Rock' — this is Abraham, as it is said: ״הביטו אל צור חצבתם״."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The text includes a long Aramaic phrase דא אילא ואימתא which appears to be a midrashic paraphrase left in Aramaic; it is rendered parenthetically. The overall comment is an extended midrash closely paralleling b. Nedarim 32a and Bereishit Rabbah 44."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:42.630470254Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}